Incident Report based off DFIR "From Bing Search to Ransomware: Bumblebee and AdaptixC2 Deliver Akira" attack analysis

1.Summary
Incident ID:0001
Incident Overview: In july 2025 intrusion was detected using SEO poisoning attack. Domain was created to lure users
searching for "ManageEngine OPManager". After downloading MSI installer DLL side-loading was used to establish 
command-and-control. Five hours later renamed AdgNsy.exe was deployed to establish persistent Command-and-control channel
to allow living-off-the-land via windows discovery commands (systeminfo, nltest). Threat actor created new privileged accounts
and installed RustDesk on multiple servers for remote access. For next two days threat actor used RDP(Remote Desktop Protocol) to
gain access to domain controller and backup server. After that credentials were harvested and LSASS memory was dumped across multiple hosts.
Data exfiltration was done through FileZilla, approximately 75 GB of data was exfiltrated including file shares, user credentials, SYSVOL domain
configurations. 44 hours after initial access Akira ransomware was deployed to maximize impact across the infrastructure.
Key findings: initial access done via SEO poisoning,file shares user credentials and SYSVOL domain configuration data exfiltrated, 
BumbleBee loader, Akira malware(locker.exe), AdaptixC2 beacon,SSH proxy, FileZilla, SFTP  used. Data was encrypted after two days
Stakeholder Impact: Exposure of customers' and clients' data, Possible legal liability, Possible loss of shareholder's value,
Exposure of employees' data

